const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — The Proposal That Was Never Accepted: Raja Vasireddy and the Making of an Insurance Contract"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What insurance is and what it is not · Definition and meaning · The subject matter of insurance · The principles that apply to every insurance contract · Formation of the contract — proposal, acceptance, premium, cover note, policy · Sec 64VB of the Insurance Act · History of insurance in England and in India in outline"));
c.push(STORY("Vijayawada, 1961. A landowner called Raja Vasireddy Chandrasekhara Prasad wanted a life policy of fifty thousand rupees. He filled in the proposal form on 27 December 1960, went to the medical examination, and paid the first premium by cheque. The Life Insurance Corporation's Divisional Office received the papers, sent them up to its Zonal Office for approval, and on 11 January 1961 the cheque was cashed. Then, on 12 January, before any letter of acceptance was written, the Raja died. His widow, Komalavalli Kamba, claimed the fifty thousand rupees. LIC refused: no policy had been issued and the proposal had never been accepted. The Andhra Pradesh High Court found for the widow — the premium had been taken, the medical passed, the risk had in substance been assumed. The Supreme Court reversed. An insurance contract, it said, is a contract like any other; it needs an offer and an acceptance, and acceptance must be communicated. Encashing the cheque was not acceptance; the Corporation's own rules required a decision by the Divisional Manager, which had not been taken. There was no contract, and therefore no claim. The widow got nothing but a refund of the premium. The case is the doorway to the whole subject, because it teaches that insurance is contract first — the special rules of good faith, insurable interest and indemnity are built on the ordinary law of offer, acceptance and consideration."));
c.push(H3("What insurance is — definition and meaning"));
c.push(P("Neither the Insurance Act, 1938 nor the Contract Act, 1872 defines 'insurance'; the Insurance Act defines the businesses (Sec 2(11) life insurance business; Sec 2(6A) fire; Sec 2(13A) marine; Sec 2(6B) general insurance business; Sec 2(9) insurer). The classic judicial definition is **Channell J. in Prudential Insurance Co v. Inland Revenue Commissioners (1904)**: a contract of insurance is one by which, for a consideration called the premium, the insurer undertakes to pay a sum of money or provide a benefit on the happening of an uncertain event — uncertain either as to whether it will happen at all (fire, theft) or as to when it will happen (death) — in which the insured has an interest. Three elements follow: **premium** (consideration), **uncertainty** of the event, and **interest** of the insured in the subject matter. Lord Mansfield's older phrase in Carter v. Boehm — insurance is 'a contract upon speculation' — captures the risk element; Lawrence J. in Lucena v. Craufurd (1806) called it a contract 'by which the one party, in consideration of a price paid to him adequate to the risk, becomes security to the other that he shall not suffer loss'. In economic terms insurance is the pooling of risk: the premiums of the many who do not suffer pay the losses of the few who do, and the insurer is the manager of the pool. Its social functions — security, mobilisation of savings, credit support, loss prevention — are why the State regulates it so closely."));
c.push(H3("The subject matter of insurance"));
c.push(P("The **subject matter of insurance** is the thing exposed to the peril — a life, a house, a ship, cargo, goods, a liability, a debt. The **subject matter of the contract** is different: it is the insured's pecuniary interest in that thing (Castellain v. Preston, 1883). The distinction matters because a person with no interest in the thing has nothing to insure, and because the amount recoverable in indemnity insurance is measured by the interest, not by the value of the thing. Insurance can be taken on anything in which a lawful interest exists: property (fire, marine, burglary, motor own-damage), person (life, personal accident, health), liability (third-party motor, public liability, professional indemnity), and pecuniary interests (fidelity guarantee, credit insurance, business interruption)."));
c.push(H3("The principles that apply to every insurance contract"));
c.push(P("Six principles run through the subject and each gets its own episode: **utmost good faith** (uberrima fides — both parties must disclose every material fact; Episode 02); **insurable interest** (the insured must stand to lose by the event; Episode 03); **indemnity** (in property and liability insurance the insured recovers only the loss, never a profit — life and personal accident are exceptions; Episode 02); **proximate cause** (the insurer pays only if the insured peril is the effective cause; Episode 04); **subrogation** (after paying, the insurer steps into the insured's shoes against third parties; Episode 04); and **contribution** (where several policies cover the same loss the insurers share it; Episode 04). Add to these the ordinary elements of a valid contract under Sec 10 of the Contract Act — competent parties, free consent, lawful consideration and object — and the special statutory rules of the Insurance Act, 1938."));
c.push(H3("Formation of the contract — step by step"));
c.push(P("**(1) Proposal.** The proposer fills in the insurer's printed **proposal form** — the questions are the insurer's way of asking for the material facts, and the form usually contains a declaration that the answers are true and form the 'basis of the contract'. Under the IRDAI (Protection of Policyholders' Interests) Regulations the proposal form must be in plain language and, if filled by the agent, the proposer must certify it. **(2) Acceptance.** The insurer accepts by issuing an acceptance letter or the policy; until then there is no contract even if the premium has been paid — **Life Insurance Corporation v. Raja Vasireddy Komalavalli Kamba (1984)**. Acceptance may be conditional (subject to medical examination, to payment of the first premium, to the proposer being in good health on the date of acceptance). A counter-proposal — acceptance at a higher premium or with an exclusion — needs the proposer's assent. **(3) Premium.** Sec 64VB of the Insurance Act: no insurer shall assume any risk unless the premium is received in advance (or a deposit is made, or a guarantee given, as the rules allow); the risk attaches only from the date the premium is received, and in life insurance the proviso allows the risk to be assumed from the date of the proposal if the premium was paid before that. **(4) Cover note.** In general insurance the insurer may issue a **cover note** — a temporary contract for a short period pending the policy; it is a contract in itself, subject to the insurer's usual terms even if they are not printed on it (**General Assurance Society v. Chandmull Jain, 1966**). **(5) Policy.** The formal document — schedule, operative clause, conditions, exclusions, warranties, endorsements. The policy is evidence of the contract, not the contract itself; a contract can exist without a policy (Chandmull Jain), and a policy can be reformed if it does not record the agreed terms. **(6) Commencement of risk**: from the date stated in the policy, never before the premium is received (Sec 64VB), and in life insurance conventionally from the date of the first premium receipt; if the life is in poor health between proposal and acceptance the proposer must disclose it (the duty of disclosure continues until the contract is concluded)."));
c.push(CASE("LIC v. Raja Vasireddy Komalavalli Kamba (1984) 2 SCC 719","No contract of insurance until the proposal is accepted and the acceptance is communicated; mere receipt and encashment of the premium is not acceptance; the insurer's internal rules on who may accept bind the proposer. The premium is refunded."));
c.push(CASE("General Assurance Society v. Chandmull Jain (1966) 3 SCR 500","A cover note is a contract of insurance for the interim period; the insurer's standard terms are incorporated; in construing a policy the court gives words their plain meaning and, in case of genuine ambiguity, reads them against the insurer who drafted them (contra proferentem); the court cannot make a new contract for the parties."));
c.push(CASE("Chandrakantaben v. LIC (1986, Gujarat) and the salary-savings cases; Delhi Electric Supply Undertaking v. Basanti Devi (1999) 8 SCC 229","Where premium is deducted from salary under a salary-savings scheme, the employer collects as LIC's agent; the policy does not lapse because the employer failed to remit, and LIC must pay the widow."));
c.push(H3("History in England — in outline"));
c.push(P("Marine insurance is the oldest: Italian merchants of Genoa and Florence in the fourteenth century; the Lombards brought it to London; the first English policy on record is of 1547; **Lloyd's** began as Edward Lloyd's coffee house in Tower Street about 1688, where underwriters wrote their names under the risk — 'underwriting' — and became a society in 1771 and a corporation by the Lloyd's Act 1871. Fire insurance was born of the Great Fire of London (1666): Nicholas Barbon's Fire Office (1680), the Sun Fire Office (1710). Life insurance began with the Amicable Society (1706) and the **Equitable Life** (1762), the first to use mortality tables and level premiums (Dodson). Parliament intervened to stop gambling in the guise of insurance: the Marine Insurance Act 1745 (no marine policy without interest), the **Life Assurance Act 1774** (the 'Gambling Act' — insurable interest in life; the name of the beneficiary in the policy). Lord Mansfield built the common law of insurance in the eighteenth century (Carter v. Boehm 1766). The **Marine Insurance Act 1906** (Chalmers) codified the marine law and is the model for the Indian Act of 1963. Modern regulation: the Assurance Companies Act 1909, the Insurance Companies Acts 1958-1982, the Financial Services Acts, the Consumer Insurance (Disclosure and Representations) Act 2012 and the Insurance Act 2015, which replaced the old all-or-nothing rules on non-disclosure and warranties with proportionate remedies."));
c.push(H3("History in India — in outline"));
c.push(P("Insurance ideas are old in India — Manu, Yajnavalkya and Kautilya speak of pooling resources against calamity; marine loans (bottomry) were known to traders. Modern insurance came with the British: the **Oriental Life Insurance Company, Calcutta, 1818** (the first life company, for Europeans; it charged Indians extra premium); the **Bombay Mutual Life Assurance Society, 1870**, the first Indian company to insure Indian lives at normal rates; the **Triton Insurance Company, Calcutta, 1850**, the first general insurer; Bharat Insurance (1896), Empire of India (1897), and the swadeshi companies after 1905 (Hindustan Co-operative, United India, National Indian, National Insurance). Legislation: the **Indian Life Assurance Companies Act 1912** (first statute, life only, following the English 1909 Act), the Indian Insurance Companies Act 1928 (statistics), the **Insurance Act 1938** (comprehensive control of both life and general business — registration, deposits, investments, agents, the Controller of Insurance), the 1950 amendment. **Nationalisation**: 245 Indian and foreign life insurers and provident societies were taken over by ordinance on 19 January 1956 and merged into the **Life Insurance Corporation of India** under the LIC Act 1956 (LIC began on 1 September 1956), partly in response to mismanagement and frauds in private companies; general insurance was nationalised by the **General Insurance Business (Nationalisation) Act 1972** — 107 insurers merged into four subsidiaries of the General Insurance Corporation (National, New India, Oriental, United India) from 1 January 1973. **Liberalisation**: the Malhotra Committee (1993, report 1994) recommended opening the sector and an independent regulator; the **Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority Act 1999** created the IRDA (now IRDAI), ended LIC's and GIC's monopolies and allowed private companies with 26 per cent foreign equity; the Insurance Laws (Amendment) Act 2015 raised the cap to 49 per cent, reformed Sec 45, agents and penalties, and let foreign reinsurers open branches; the 2021 amendment raised it to 74 per cent; the GIBNA amendment of 2021 allowed the Government to reduce its stake in the public general insurers; LIC's shares were listed in May 2022. Today there are about two dozen life and three dozen general and health insurers, GIC Re and foreign reinsurance branches, all under the IRDAI, with the Insurance Ombudsman and the consumer commissions handling policyholder disputes."));
c.push(TBL([
["Year","England","India"],
["1666-1710","Great Fire; Fire Office; Sun Fire Office","—"],
["1688 / 1771","Lloyd's coffee house; Society of Lloyd's","—"],
["1745 / 1774","Marine Insurance Act; Life Assurance Act (interest required)","—"],
["1818 / 1850 / 1870","—","Oriental Life; Triton (general); Bombay Mutual"],
["1906","Marine Insurance Act (Chalmers)","—"],
["1912 / 1928 / 1938","—","Life Assurance Companies Act; Insurance Companies Act; Insurance Act"],
["1956 / 1972","—","LIC Act (life nationalised); GIBNA (general nationalised)"],
["1963","—","Marine Insurance Act"],
["1988","—","Motor Vehicles Act (Chapter XI compulsory insurance)"],
["1999 / 2015 / 2021","—","IRDA Act; FDI 49% and Sec 45 reform; FDI 74%; LIC listed 2022"],
["2012 / 2015","Consumer Insurance Act; Insurance Act 2015","—"]
],[1600,3700,3700]));
c.push(DIG("Proposal forms are now filled online, premium is paid electronically and the policy is issued in dematerialised form through insurance repositories (IRDAI mandates e-policies for most classes since 2016 and the 'Bima Sugam' portal is being built as a common marketplace). Sec 64VB is satisfied by electronic receipt; the Information Technology Act, 2000 recognises the electronic proposal and signature, though Sec 1(4) of that Act with its Schedule originally excluded some instruments — insurance policies are not among the exclusions."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Define insurance', 'Subject matter of insurance', 'Cover note', 'Sec 64VB', 'Oriental Life Insurance Company 1818', 'Nationalisation of life insurance 1956'. Part B: 'Define insurance and explain the essential elements of a contract of insurance and how it is formed' — Channell J.'s three elements, then proposal, acceptance (Raja Vasireddy), premium (Sec 64VB), cover note (Chandmull Jain), policy; or 'Trace the history of insurance in England and India' — the table. Part C: a proposer dies after paying the premium but before acceptance — no contract (Raja Vasireddy), premium refunded; an agent accepts a proposal on the spot — agents have no authority to accept, so no contract until the insurer accepts; a fire occurs during the currency of a cover note — insurer liable on its standard terms (Chandmull Jain)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — The Fort and the Frenchman: Carter v Boehm, Utmost Good Faith and the Nature of the Contract"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Uberrima fides — the duty of disclosure and Sec 45 · Material facts · Non-disclosure and misrepresentation · Warranties and the basis clause · Contract of indemnity · Insurance and wager · Conditional (contingent) contract · Classification of insurance · Construction of policies"));
c.push(STORY("Sumatra, 1760. George Carter was Governor of Fort Marlborough, the East India Company's trading post at Bencoolen. He knew the fort was weak — built to resist the natives, not a European army — and he had heard the French were coming. He wrote to his brother in London and had a policy taken out with the underwriter Charles Boehm: a thousand pounds against the loss of the fort to a foreign enemy within a year. In April 1760 the French Count d'Estaing sailed in, and the fort fell. Boehm refused to pay, saying Carter had concealed the fort's weakness and the likelihood of attack. Lord Mansfield, sitting with a special jury of merchants, laid down the rule that has governed insurance ever since: insurance is a contract upon speculation; the facts lie mostly in the knowledge of the insured; the underwriter trusts his representation; the concealment of a material fact, even by mistake and without fraud, avoids the policy. And yet Carter won. The underwriter, Mansfield said, must be presumed to know what everybody knows — that a trading fort is not a fortress, that in wartime the French might attack — and he need not be told what he could have found out; the insured need not disclose the general political situation or the underwriter's own bad guess about the odds. Two rules were born in one judgment: the duty of utmost good faith, and its limits. Every non-disclosure case since, from Mithoolal Nayak to Rekhaben Rathod, is an argument about where those limits lie."));
c.push(H3("Uberrima fides — the duty of utmost good faith"));
c.push(P("Ordinary contracts are governed by caveat emptor; there is no general duty to volunteer information (Sec 17 Explanation, Contract Act — mere silence is not fraud unless there is a duty to speak). Insurance is the great exception: it is a contract **uberrimae fidei** — of the utmost good faith — because the insured alone knows the facts that determine the risk. The duty has two branches. **Disclosure**: the proposer must disclose every **material fact** known to him, whether asked or not, before the contract is concluded (and again on renewal, and on revival of a lapsed policy); a fact is material if it would influence the judgment of a prudent insurer in deciding whether to take the risk and at what premium (Sec 20(2) of the Marine Insurance Act 1963, borrowed by all branches; **Satwant Kaur Sandhu v. New India Assurance, 2009**). **Representation**: statements actually made must be true; a material misrepresentation, even an innocent one, entitles the insurer to avoid. The duty is mutual — the insurer too must disclose (Carter v. Boehm; Banque Financière v. Westgate, 1990) and must not mislead about the cover — but in practice it bites on the insured. What need not be disclosed (Carter v. Boehm; Sec 20(3) MIA): facts that diminish the risk; facts the insurer knows or ought to know in the ordinary course of business; facts the insurer waives (by not asking, or by leaving a question unanswered without objection); facts covered by a warranty. Facts that are material in life insurance: age, previous illnesses, hospitalisation, habits, occupation, other proposals declined or rated up, existing policies (Rekhaben); in fire: construction, occupancy, previous fires, previous refusals; in motor: previous accidents and claims, licence, use; in marine: the vessel's condition, the voyage, the nature of the cargo."));
c.push(H3("Sec 45 of the Insurance Act, 1938 — the life insurer's window"));
c.push(P("The old Sec 45 allowed an insurer to call a life policy in question after two years only if it proved that the statement was on a material matter, was fraudulently made, and was known to the insured to be false — the three conditions of **Mithoolal Nayak v. LIC (1962)** and **LIC v. Asha Goel (2001)**. The **Insurance Laws (Amendment) Act 2015** rewrote it. **Sec 45(1)**: no policy of life insurance shall be called in question on any ground whatever after three years from the date of the policy, of commencement of risk, of revival, or of the rider, whichever is latest. **Sec 45(2)**: within those three years the insurer may repudiate for **fraud** — an act with intent to deceive: a false suggestion, active concealment with knowledge, any other deceit, or an act the law declares fraudulent — provided it states the grounds in writing to the insured or the legal representatives, and the burden of proving fraud lies on the insurer; mere silence is not fraud unless the circumstances raised a duty to speak or silence is itself equivalent to speech, and the insured (or the nominees) may prove that the misstatement was true to the best of the insured's knowledge and belief or that the insurer knew the truth. **Sec 45(4)**: within the three years the insurer may also repudiate for a misstatement or suppression of a material fact not amounting to fraud, but must refund the premiums paid within ninety days of repudiation, and the onus of proving materiality is on the insurer. **Sec 45(5)**: a proved misstatement of age may still be adjusted after three years. The section thus creates a strict incontestability after three years and puts the burden squarely on the insurer inside it."));
c.push(H3("Warranties, the basis clause and conditions"));
c.push(P("A **warranty** in insurance is a promise by the insured that a fact is or will be true, or that something will or will not be done; unlike a warranty in the sale of goods it is a condition precedent to the insurer's liability — breach discharges the insurer whether or not the fact was material or connected with the loss (Sec 35 MIA 1963; Dawsons v. Bonnin, 1922 — a lorry garaged at the wrong address; Anderson v. Fitzgerald, 1853). A **'basis of the contract' clause** in the proposal form turns every answer into a warranty, so that an inaccurate answer, however trivial, avoids the policy — a doctrine so harsh that England abolished it for consumers in 2012 and for all in 2015. In India the courts soften it by insisting that the statement be shown to be false and material, and by treating ambiguous answers against the insurer; the IRDAI regulations require the proposal form to warn the proposer, and Sec 45 caps the life insurer's remedy. **Conditions** in the policy are of three kinds: conditions precedent to the contract (payment of premium), conditions precedent to liability (notice of loss within the stipulated time, no admission of liability to third parties, proof of loss), and mere conditions whose breach sounds only in damages. Whether a delay in intimation is fatal depends on whether the condition is precedent and on the prejudice caused — **Sonell Clocks v. National Insurance (2018)** (condition precedent; delay of 25 days after knowledge fatal), against **Gurshinder Singh v. Shriram General Insurance (2020)** (theft: prompt FIR, delay in informing insurer not fatal) and **Galada Power v. United India (2016)**."));
c.push(H3("Contract of indemnity"));
c.push(P("Property, marine and liability insurance are contracts of **indemnity**: the insured is to be placed in the same position after the loss as before, and no better (Castellain v. Preston). The sum insured is a ceiling, not the measure; the insured recovers the actual loss, subject to average, excess and the policy limits. The Contract Act's Sec 124 defines indemnity narrowly — protection against loss caused by the conduct of the promisor or another person — and does not in terms cover loss by fire or sea (Gajanan Moreshwar v. Moreshwar Madan, 1942 Bombay, applied the wider English equitable rule); insurance indemnity is therefore a creature of the common law read into the contract. From indemnity flow **subrogation** (the insurer takes the insured's rights against the wrongdoer so the insured is not paid twice), **contribution** (co-insurers share the loss so the insured is not paid twice), the rule against over-insurance, the salvage rule (the insurer who pays a total loss takes what is left) and the reinstatement option. **Life insurance and personal accident insurance are not contracts of indemnity** (Dalby v. India and London Life Assurance, 1854): a life has no market value, the sum assured is payable in full whatever the loss, several policies may be taken and all paid, and there is no subrogation — hence the widow of a man killed in a road accident recovers both the life policy and damages from the driver."));
c.push(H3("Insurance and wager"));
c.push(P("A **wager** (Sec 30 Contract Act, void) is an agreement in which two persons with opposite views on an uncertain event agree that one will pay the other depending on the outcome, neither having any other interest in the event. Insurance is superficially similar — payment on an uncertain event — but differs in four ways: the insured has an **insurable interest** (he does not gain by the event, he is protected against loss); the contract is one of **indemnity** (except life, where interest at inception serves the same purpose); the premium is scientifically calculated on the risk, not a stake; and the contract is socially useful and enforced by law, whereas a wager is void. Without insurable interest a policy is a wager and void: Sec 6 of the Marine Insurance Act 1963 says so expressly ('every contract of marine insurance by way of gambling or wagering is void'), the Life Assurance Act 1774 says so for life in England, and the general rule of Sec 30 does the rest in India. A policy 'interest or no interest' or 'without benefit of salvage' (p.p.i. policies) is a wager (Sec 6(2) MIA)."));
c.push(H3("Conditional contracts"));
c.push(P("Insurance is a **contingent contract** under Sec 31 of the Contract Act — a contract to do something if an uncertain event collateral to the contract does or does not happen — and Secs 32-36 govern it: enforceable only when the event happens (Sec 32), not enforceable if the event becomes impossible (Sec 32), void if made on an impossible event (Sec 36). The insurer's promise is conditional on the peril occurring within the policy period, on the conditions being performed, and on the exclusions not applying. Conditional also describes the cover note's 'subject to' clauses and the acceptance 'subject to first premium and good health'."));
c.push(H3("Classification of insurance"));
c.push(P("**By subject matter**: (a) **life insurance** (Sec 2(11)) — on human life, with annuity, disability and health riders; (b) **general insurance** (Sec 2(6B)) — fire, marine and miscellaneous (motor, burglary, personal accident, health, liability, fidelity, credit, engineering, crop, livestock, aviation, cyber); the 1938 Act adds (c) **health insurance business** (Sec 2(6C), 2015) as a separate class. **By nature of the promise**: contracts of indemnity (property, liability) and contingency contracts (life, personal accident). **By the persons insured**: individual, group (employees, borrowers), social insurance (ESI, PMJJBY, PMSBY, Ayushman Bharat, crop insurance under PMFBY). **By the parties' relationship**: direct insurance, co-insurance (several insurers each take a share on one policy), double insurance (the same interest insured with two insurers), and reinsurance (the insurer insures its own liability). **By duration and valuation**: time and voyage policies; valued and unvalued policies; floating and declaration policies. **By the risk**: named-perils policies (standard fire), all-risks policies (jewellery, contractors' all risks, marine 'A' clauses), and comprehensive policies (motor package)."));
c.push(H3("Construction of policies"));
c.push(P("A policy is construed like any commercial document, with rules the courts have refined: (1) the **intention of the parties** is gathered from the whole document — schedule, operative clause, conditions, exclusions, endorsements read together; (2) words bear their **ordinary meaning**, technical words their technical meaning, and words are read in their context; (3) written or typed words prevail over printed ones, and later endorsements over earlier terms; (4) **contra proferentem** — genuine ambiguity is resolved against the insurer who drafted the policy (Chandmull Jain), but the court cannot rewrite a clear term to relieve hardship — the policy is to be read strictly, neither adding nor subtracting (**Oriental Insurance v. Sony Cheriyan, 1999**; **Vikram Greentech v. New India, 2009**; **Export Credit Guarantee Corporation v. Garg Sons International, 2013** — exclusions and conditions must be given effect; **United India v. Pushpalaya Printers, 2004** — 'impact by rail/road vehicle' read to cover damage from a bulldozer's vibration, the wider reading preferred where two are reasonable); (5) exclusion clauses are read narrowly and the insurer must prove that the loss falls within them; (6) the **reasonable expectation** of the insured is relevant where the insurer's own literature created it (**Jacob Punnen v. United India, 2022** — insurer must inform the insured of a change in the renewed policy's terms); (7) in consumer fora the same rules apply, and repudiation on a ground not stated in the letter of repudiation is not allowed (Saurashtra Chemicals v. National Insurance, 2019)."));
c.push(CASE("Carter v. Boehm (1766) 3 Burr 1905","Insurance is a contract of utmost good faith; concealment of a material fact avoids the policy; but the insurer is presumed to know matters of general knowledge and what he could have discovered, and need not be told what diminishes the risk."));
c.push(CASE("Mithoolal Nayak v. LIC, AIR 1962 SC 814","Mahajan Deolal concealed that he had been treated for anaemia and tuberculosis; the policy was avoided; under the old Sec 45 the insurer had to show a material misstatement, fraudulently made, with knowledge of its falsity; premiums forfeited."));
c.push(CASE("LIC v. Asha Goel (2001) 2 SCC 160","A writ against LIC's repudiation is maintainable in a fit case (LIC is 'State'); the three Sec 45 conditions restated; the insurer must prove them and must not repudiate on flimsy grounds — the widow won."));
c.push(CASE("Satwant Kaur Sandhu v. New India Assurance (2009) 8 SCC 316","A 'material fact' is one that would influence a prudent insurer; a proposer who concealed chronic diabetes and renal failure in a mediclaim proposal could not recover; the duty of disclosure is the foundation of the contract."));
c.push(CASE("Reliance Life Insurance v. Rekhaben Nareshbhai Rathod (2019) 6 SCC 175","Non-disclosure of an existing life policy with another insurer was a material fact; the proposal form is the basis of the contract; the insurer's repudiation within the Sec 45 window was upheld and the consumer forum's order set aside."));
c.push(CASE("Manmohan Nanda v. United India (2022) 4 SCC 582","Where the insurer accepted the proposal after its own medical tests and had the opportunity to detect the condition, repudiation of an overseas medical claim for 'non-disclosure' of hyperlipidaemia was not permitted; a diagnosis the proposer did not know of is not concealed."));
c.push(DIG("Insurers now check medical and claims history through the Insurance Information Bureau and, for motor, the Vahan database and the claims-history register; non-disclosure of a previous claim shows up at renewal. The IRDAI's 2024 master circular on health insurance shortens the moratorium period after which no claim can be contested for non-disclosure to five years of continuous cover, mirroring Sec 45's logic for health policies."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Uberrima fides', 'Material fact', 'Warranty in insurance', 'Basis of the contract clause', 'Sec 45', 'Insurance vs wager', 'Contra proferentem'. Part B: 'Insurance is a contract of utmost good faith — explain with reference to the duty of disclosure, its scope and exceptions, and the effect of Sec 45 as amended in 2015' — Carter v. Boehm, material fact (Satwant Kaur), the exceptions, Sec 45(1)-(5) step by step, Mithoolal Nayak, Asha Goel, Rekhaben; or 'Distinguish a contract of insurance from a wager and explain why life insurance is not a contract of indemnity'; or 'State the rules of construction of an insurance policy'. Part C: a policyholder dies in the fourth year and the insurer discovers he concealed heart disease — cannot repudiate, Sec 45(1); dies in the second year after concealing an earlier declined proposal — repudiation for fraud if the insurer proves intent, else refund of premiums under Sec 45(4); a fire policy warranty says goods will be stored in a brick building and they are moved to a shed — insurer discharged (Dawsons v. Bonnin), no need to show the shed caused the fire; an exclusion is ambiguous — read against the insurer (Chandmull Jain), but a clear exclusion binds (Garg Sons)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — The Timber That Belonged to the Company: Macaura v Northern Assurance, Insurable Interest, Premium and Risk"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Insurable interest — meaning, when required, in life, property, liability · Lucena v Craufurd, Dalby, Macaura · Indian position · Premium — payment, Sec 64VB, days of grace, return of premium · Risk — nature, commencement, alteration, increase · Assignment of the policy and of the subject matter (Sec 38 Insurance Act; Sec 49 Transfer of Property Act)"));
c.push(STORY("County Tyrone, Ireland, 1919. Mr Macaura owned the Killymoon estate and its timber. He sold the timber to a company, the Irish Canadian Sawmills Limited, taking payment in shares — he ended up owning every share, and the company owed him money besides. The felled timber lay on his land. He insured it against fire, in his own name, with the Northern Assurance and four other companies. Two weeks later the timber burned. The insurers refused to pay, and when arbitration found that Macaura himself had set the fire, that finding was later set aside; but the House of Lords never needed it. Lord Buckmaster and Lord Sumner held that Macaura had no insurable interest in the timber at all. The timber belonged to the company, and a company is a person distinct from its shareholders (Salomon's case). As a shareholder he had an interest in the company's profits, not in its assets; as a creditor he had a claim against the company, not against the timber; if the timber burned he would lose money only indirectly, through the fall in the value of his shares and the company's inability to pay him, and the law of insurable interest asks for a legal or equitable relation to the thing insured, not a mere expectation of loss. The policy was a wager, and void. Macaura is the case every examiner uses to show that insurable interest is a legal category, not a moral one — and it is the case that shows why the Marine Insurance Act's definition of interest, borrowed by the general law, asks about a 'legal or equitable relation'."));
c.push(H3("Insurable interest — what it is"));
c.push(P("**Insurable interest** is the insured's stake in the subject matter — such a relation to it that he benefits from its safety and is prejudiced by its loss. The classic definitions: Lawrence J. in **Lucena v. Craufurd (1806)** — 'a right in the property, or a right derivable out of some contract about the property, which in either case may be lost upon some contingency affecting the possession or enjoyment of the party'; and **Sec 7 of the Marine Insurance Act 1963** (Sec 5 of the English Act) — a person has an insurable interest 'where he stands in any legal or equitable relation to the adventure or to any insurable property at risk therein, in consequence of which he may benefit by the safety or due arrival of insurable property, or may be prejudiced by its loss, or by damage thereto, or by the detention thereof, or may incur liability in respect thereof'. The three tests are therefore: (1) a physical object or a life exposed to a peril; (2) the insured's relation to it must be legal or equitable — ownership, possession, a contract, a liability — not a mere expectation; (3) the insured must stand to gain by its safety or lose by its loss. **Why it is required**: to prevent wagering (Sec 30 Contract Act; Sec 6 MIA), to remove the temptation to destroy the subject matter (the 'moral hazard'), and to make indemnity measurable."));
c.push(H3("When the interest must exist"));
c.push(P("**Life insurance**: at the time the policy is taken; it need not exist at the time of death (**Dalby v. India and London Life Assurance, 1854** — a creditor's policy on the Duke of Cambridge's life stayed good after the debt was repaid; the earlier rule in Godsall v. Boddington, 1806, that the creditor could recover only what he was owed at death, was overruled). Life insurance is not indemnity, so once validly taken the policy is property and can be kept up and assigned. **Fire and other property insurance**: at the time of the loss (and, on the better view, at inception as well — Sadler's Co v. Badcock, 1743) — because the contract is one of indemnity and the insured must actually suffer a loss; a person who has sold the house before the fire cannot recover (Rayner v. Preston, 1881; Castellain v. Preston, 1883, where the vendor recovered but had to hold the money for the buyer's benefit), and Sec 49 of the Transfer of Property Act gives the transferee of insured property the benefit of the policy money to be applied to reinstatement in the absence of a contrary contract. **Marine insurance**: at the time of the loss, not necessarily when the insurance is effected (Sec 8(1) MIA 1963); 'lost or not lost' policies cover a loss that has already happened unknown to the parties (Sec 8(2)); a person who has no interest at the time of loss cannot acquire one afterwards. **Liability insurance**: the interest is the exposure to liability; it exists throughout."));
c.push(H3("Who has an insurable interest — life"));
c.push(P("**In one's own life**: unlimited — a person may insure his life for any amount; the premium is the only ceiling. **Husband and wife**: each in the other's life, unlimited, by the fact of the relationship (Griffiths v. Fleming, 1909; Sec 6 of the Married Women's Property Act 1874 lets a husband insure for the benefit of wife and children as a trust). **Parent and child**: at common law neither has an interest in the other's life merely as such (Halford v. Kymer, 1830) unless there is a pecuniary dependence; Indian practice allows a parent to insure a minor child's life and children to insure parents on whom they depend, and insurers in fact issue such policies on proof of relationship and financial dependence. **Creditor in the debtor's life**: to the extent of the debt with interest and premiums (Dalby). **Surety in the principal debtor's life**: to the extent of the guarantee. **Employer in the employee's life** (keyman insurance) and **partners in each other's lives**: to the extent of the pecuniary loss their death would cause. **Trustee in the beneficiary's life**, a person in the life of one who has agreed to maintain him. In every case except one's own and spouse's life, the interest must be pecuniary and provable at inception."));
c.push(H3("Who has an insurable interest — property"));
c.push(P("**Owner**: to the full value, including a co-owner to the full value if he insures for all. **Mortgagor and mortgagee**: each separately — the mortgagor as owner, the mortgagee to the extent of the debt (and a mortgagee who insures may claim against the property under Sec 72 TPA for the premium); a bank that holds goods in hypothecation is a beneficiary under the policy in its own right (**Canara Bank v. United India Insurance, 2020** — the bank as beneficiary/hypothecatee could claim; the insurer's surveyor process bound it). **Bailee**: warehouseman, carrier, repairer — in goods held, to their full value, holding the surplus for the owner (Waters v. Monarch, 1856; Hepburn v. Tomlinson, 1966). **Lessee and tenant**: in the leasehold and in fixtures. **Pledgee and lien-holder**: to the extent of the debt. **Trustee, executor, receiver, liquidator**: in the property they hold. **Purchaser under a contract of sale**: from the date the risk passes (Sec 26 Sale of Goods Act); the vendor keeps an interest until the price is paid. **Contractor**: in the works. **Shareholder in the company's property: none** (Macaura); **creditor in the debtor's property: none**, unless he holds security over it. **Agent**: may insure for the principal, who can adopt the policy. Consignor and consignee; the finder of goods; a person in lawful possession — all have sufficient interest."));
c.push(H3("Premium"));
c.push(P("The **premium** is the consideration for the insurer's promise — a price calculated by actuarial or statistical estimate of the probability and severity of the loss, plus expenses and profit; in life insurance it is a level annual premium based on mortality tables and an assumed interest rate, and in general insurance a rate per cent on the sum insured, once fixed by the Tariff Advisory Committee and since 2007 de-tariffed and set by each insurer under IRDAI rules (motor third-party premium is still notified by the IRDAI). **Sec 64VB Insurance Act** — no risk without prior receipt of premium: the payment must reach the insurer (or its authorised agent, or the bank) before the risk attaches; a policy issued against an unpaid cheque is void ab initio, and the courts have refused claims where the cheque bounced (National Insurance v. Seema Malhotra, 2001 — cheque dishonoured, insurer not liable; contrast Oriental Insurance v. Inderjit Kaur, 1998 — where the insurer had issued the certificate under the Motor Vehicles Act it remained liable to the third party even though the cheque bounced, subject to recovery from the owner). **Days of grace**: life policies allow thirty days (fifteen for monthly mode) for renewal premium; the policy continues in force during the grace period and a claim within it is payable less the premium; after the grace period the policy lapses, subject to revival within the period the policy allows (usually five years) on proof of good health and payment of arrears with interest, and subject to the non-forfeiture provisions (paid-up value and surrender value after the policy has run the minimum term — Sec 113 Insurance Act, now surrender value under the IRDAI (Non-linked Insurance Products) Regulations). **Return of premium**: the premium is returned where the risk never attached (Sec 84-86 MIA, applied generally) — the subject matter was never at risk, the policy was void for want of interest without fraud, the insurer avoided for innocent non-disclosure (Sec 45(4) mandates a refund); it is forfeited where the insured was fraudulent or the risk has once attached; a proportionate return is made on cancellation under the policy's cancellation clause (short-period scale if the insured cancels, pro rata if the insurer does)."));
c.push(H3("Risk"));
c.push(P("**Risk** is the chance of loss from the insured peril; the insurer 'runs the risk' from the moment it attaches. The **description of the risk** in the policy — the peril insured against, the subject matter, the location, the period, the use — defines the insurer's promise. **Commencement**: from the date and time stated, never before premium (Sec 64VB), and in marine insurance from the moment the goods leave the warehouse (transit clause) or the ship sails (Sec 44 MIA: the adventure must commence within a reasonable time). **Alteration and increase of risk**: the insured must not, without the insurer's consent, alter the risk in a way that increases it — fire policies contain an express condition that the cover ceases if the trade or occupancy changes, the building is altered, or the interest passes otherwise than by will or operation of law, unless the insurer agrees in writing; motor policies exclude use for a purpose not permitted by the permit; marine policies treat deviation and change of voyage as discharging the insurer (Episode 07). At common law a mere increase in risk by the insured's later conduct does not by itself discharge a fire insurer unless the policy so provides (Baxendale v. Harvey, 1859 — installing a steam engine); the policy always so provides. **Change of circumstances after the contract** need not be disclosed unless the policy requires it, but at renewal the duty of disclosure revives. **Excepted risks**: war, nuclear, wilful act of the insured, wear and tear, inherent vice, delay — standard exclusions in every branch."));
c.push(H3("Assignment"));
c.push(P("Two things can be assigned: the **policy** (a chose in action) and the **subject matter**. **Life policies**: Sec 38 of the Insurance Act (as substituted in 2015) — a transfer or assignment, wholly or in part, with or without consideration, by endorsement on the policy or by a separate instrument signed by the transferor and attested by at least one witness, stating the reasons and the antecedents of the assignee; the insurer may refuse to act on it if it is not bona fide, not in the policyholder's interest, made for trading in the policy, or is a wager, recording reasons in writing within thirty days, and the aggrieved party may appeal to the IRDAI; the assignment takes effect against the insurer only on notice; a conditional assignment (automatically reverting on the assignee's death or the insured's survival) is permitted; a partial assignment is confined to the part; and the assignee alone can give a discharge. A life policy is freely assignable because it is not a contract of indemnity and the assignee need not have an insurable interest. **Nomination** (Sec 39) is different — Episode 04. **Fire and other indemnity policies**: assignable only with the insurer's consent, because the identity of the insured is material to the risk; an assignment of the policy without the property is void, and a transfer of the property without the policy ends the policy (the fire policy's condition); the buyer of a house does not get the seller's policy unless the insurer endorses it, though under Sec 49 TPA he is entitled to have the money applied to reinstatement. **Motor policies**: Sec 157 of the Motor Vehicles Act deems the certificate of insurance and policy to be transferred with the vehicle, and the transferee must apply within fourteen days for the insurer to note the transfer — third-party cover follows the vehicle. **Marine policies**: freely assignable by endorsement before or after loss unless the policy prohibits it (Sec 52 MIA 1963), but an assured who has lost his interest cannot then assign (Sec 53)."));
c.push(CASE("Lucena v. Craufurd (1806) 2 Bos & PNR 269","Insurable interest defined; the Crown's commissioners who were to take charge of captured Dutch ships had no interest before the ships reached British ports; Lawrence J.'s 'right in the property or right derivable out of a contract' test."));
c.push(CASE("Dalby v. India and London Life Assurance Co (1854) 15 CB 365","Life insurance is not a contract of indemnity; insurable interest need exist only when the policy is taken; the policy remains valid though the interest has ceased."));
c.push(CASE("Macaura v. Northern Assurance Co (1925) AC 619","A sole shareholder and creditor has no insurable interest in the company's timber; interest must be a legal or equitable relation to the thing, not a factual expectation of loss."));
c.push(CASE("Castellain v. Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380","Indemnity is the controlling principle of fire insurance; every rule (subrogation, salvage, contribution) exists to prevent the insured recovering more than the loss; the vendor who had been paid the full price by the purchaser must return the insurance money."));
c.push(CASE("New India Assurance v. G.N. Sainani (1997) 6 SCC 383","The buyer of goods under CIF terms had insurable interest in the goods once the risk passed; the insurer's plea that the claimant had no interest failed — Indian courts apply the MIA test of legal relation."));
c.push(DIG("IRDAI's e-insurance account rules let a policyholder assign or nominate online with e-signature; the insurer must register the assignment within the Sec 38 timelines and record it in the repository. Motor policy transfers are now validated against the Vahan register, so the fourteen-day rule under Sec 157 is enforced automatically at renewal."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Insurable interest', 'Macaura's case', 'Dalby v. India and London Life', 'Days of grace', 'Sec 64VB', 'Return of premium', 'Assignment of a life policy'. Part B: 'Explain the doctrine of insurable interest, when it must exist in life, fire and marine insurance, and who has such interest' — the definition (Lucena, Sec 7 MIA), the timing rule with cases, then the lists; or 'Discuss the law relating to assignment of insurance policies under the Insurance Act and the Transfer of Property Act'. Part C: a shareholder insures the company's factory in his own name — void (Macaura); a wife's policy on her husband's life continues after divorce — valid (Dalby); a man insures his neighbour's house — wager, void; a house sold after a fire policy is taken burns before completion — the seller can claim but holds for the buyer (Castellain) and the buyer may claim the money for reinstatement (Sec 49 TPA); the premium cheque bounces after the policy issues — no risk (Seema Malhotra) except third-party motor (Inderjit Kaur)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Ship That Sank in Harbour: Leyland Shipping, Proximate Cause, Subrogation and Contribution"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Proximate cause — the rule, its tests, its application · Non-disclosure and representations recapped · Nomination under Sec 39 · Contribution · Subrogation · Double insurance and over-insurance · Reinsurance"));
c.push(STORY("The English Channel, January 1915. The steamship Ikaria, insured against perils of the sea but with war risks excluded, was torpedoed by a German submarine twenty-five miles from Le Havre. Tugs towed her into the harbour and she was moored at a quay. There, taking water, she threatened to sink and block the berth, so the port authority ordered her out to a berth inside the outer breakwater. There she lay for two days, grounding at each ebb tide and lifting at each flood, until the strain of the tides broke her back and she sank. The owners claimed against the underwriters for a loss by perils of the sea — the last thing that happened to her was that the sea broke her. The House of Lords refused. Lord Shaw explained why: causation is not a chain but a net; the question is not which cause came last but which was 'proximate in efficiency' — the dominant, operative, effective cause. The torpedo had never stopped operating; everything that followed, the towing, the mooring, the grounding, was the torpedo's work still going on. The proximate cause was the war peril, and the war peril was excluded. Leyland Shipping v Norwich Union is the standard authority on the doctrine of causa proxima non remota spectatur, and it is the reason a modern examiner will give you a fact-pattern of three linked events and ask 'who pays?'"));
c.push(H3("Proximate cause — the rule"));
c.push(P("**Causa proxima non remota spectatur** — the proximate, not the remote, cause is regarded. **Sec 57(1) of the Marine Insurance Act 1963** (Sec 55 of the English Act): the insurer is liable for any loss proximately caused by a peril insured against, and not liable for any loss not so caused; **Sec 57(2)**: the insurer is not liable for loss attributable to the wilful misconduct of the assured, nor (unless the policy otherwise provides) for loss caused by delay even though the delay is caused by an insured peril, nor for ordinary wear and tear, leakage and breakage, inherent vice, or rats and vermin. The rule applies to every branch of insurance. **What 'proximate' means**: not the cause nearest in time but the cause that is dominant and effective — 'proximate in efficiency' (**Leyland Shipping v. Norwich Union, 1918**); Pawsey v. Scottish Union (1907): 'the active, efficient cause that sets in motion a train of events which brings about a result, without the intervention of any force started and working actively from a new and independent source'. **The tests in practice**: (1) Where perils operate **in sequence** and each is the natural consequence of the one before, the first is proximate — a fire causes an explosion which causes a wall to fall on a neighbour's goods: fire is the cause; but if a new and independent cause intervenes and breaks the chain, the new cause is proximate — a fire drives a crowd into the street and a mob loots a shop: the loss is by riot, not fire (Marsden v. City and County Insurance, 1865 — plate glass broken by the mob, not by the fire). (2) Where an insured peril and an excepted peril operate **concurrently** and neither is dominant, the loss falls outside the policy if the excepted peril is excluded (Wayne Tank v. Employers' Liability, 1974), but inside it if the other peril is merely uninsured rather than excluded. (3) Where the insured peril is the **reasonable and necessary consequence** of trying to avoid another peril — a ship runs aground to escape capture — the loss is by the peril avoided (the 'necessity' cases); a ship's crew, weakened by a war peril, fails to save her from a sea peril: the war peril is proximate (the 'Coxwold' line of cases). (4) **Delay, wear and tear and inherent vice** are never proximate causes of an insured loss unless the policy says so. (5) Where the loss is caused by the **insured's own deliberate act** it is not a fortuity and is excluded (arson; suicide within the excluded period; scuttling)."));
c.push(P("**Indian applications.** **New India Assurance v. Zuari Industries (2009) 9 SCC 70** — a fire in a turbine generator's lubricating oil system led to an explosion that destroyed the turbo-generator; the insurer said the explosion (not covered) not the fire (covered) caused the loss; the Supreme Court held fire was the proximate cause of the damage and the insurer must pay. **Oriental Insurance v. Samayanallur Primary Agricultural Co-operative Bank (2000)** — goods hypothecated to the bank were destroyed when the shed collapsed after heavy rains; storm and flood were covered perils; the insurer's plea of poor construction was rejected. **Sikka Papers v. National Insurance (2009) 7 SCC 777** — a fire caused by burning of waste on the insured's own premises was still loss by fire; but the insured's own negligence not amounting to wilful misconduct does not defeat the claim (negligence is one of the things one insures against). **Alka Shukla v. LIC (2019)** — a heart attack while riding a scooter is not an 'accident' as the proximate cause of death; the accident benefit rider was not payable (Episode 09). **Smt. Rita Devi v. New India (2000)** — an auto-rickshaw driver murdered by passengers who stole the vehicle died in an 'accident arising out of the use of the motor vehicle' because the murder was incidental to the theft; contrast a murder that merely happens to occur in a vehicle."));
c.push(H3("Non-disclosure and representations — the checklist"));
c.push(P("Episode 02 gives the law; here is the checklist a question expects. (1) Was the fact material — would a prudent insurer's judgment be influenced? (2) Was it known to the proposer, or one he ought to have known in the ordinary course? (3) Did the insurer know it, or ought it to have? (4) Did the insurer waive it — an unanswered question left unanswered, a medical examination that would have revealed it (Manmohan Nanda)? (5) Was the non-disclosure induced the contract (in England now required; in India the test remains materiality plus, for life policies, the Sec 45 framework)? (6) Timing — within Sec 45's three years for life; for other classes the insurer may avoid ab initio at any time but the consumer fora look for prejudice and promptness. (7) Remedy — avoidance ab initio with return of premium for innocent non-disclosure (Sec 45(4) makes the refund compulsory in life), forfeiture for fraud (Sec 45(2)); the insurer cannot affirm the policy (accept premiums after knowledge) and then avoid it."));
c.push(H3("Nomination — Sec 39"));
c.push(P("A **nomination** is the policyholder's direction that the money secured by the policy be paid to a named person on his death (**Sec 39 Insurance Act**): made in the policy or by endorsement, notified to the insurer and registered; may be cancelled or changed by endorsement; a minor nominee needs an appointee; a transfer or assignment automatically cancels a nomination (except an assignment to the insurer for a loan, or a conditional assignment that reverts). The nominee's legal position was fixed by **Sarbati Devi v. Usha Devi (1984) 1 SCC 424**: a nominee is only a person authorised to receive and give a valid discharge; the money belongs to the estate and passes to the heirs by succession; nomination is not a will and confers no beneficial interest. The **2015 amendment** added **Sec 39(7)-(8)**: where the nominee is a parent, spouse or child (a 'beneficial nominee'), the nominee is beneficially entitled to the amount payable, unless the policy or the law expressly says otherwise — so, for policies issued after 26 December 2014, a spouse or child nominee keeps the money; other nominees remain mere receivers under Sarbati Devi. (Shakti Yezdani v. Jayanand Jayant Salgaonkar, 2023, reaffirms the Sarbati Devi principle for nominees of shares under the Companies Act.) Nomination is available only for life policies (and policies on the nominator's own life); it differs from assignment in that the policyholder keeps control, can change it at will, and the nominee gets nothing while the policyholder lives."));
c.push(H3("Double insurance and contribution"));
c.push(P("**Double insurance** exists where the same insured insures the same interest in the same subject matter against the same peril with two or more insurers, and the total sums insured exceed the value (Sec 34 MIA 1963). It is lawful; the insured may claim from the insurers in any order he chooses but cannot recover in all more than the loss (the indemnity principle); each insurer is liable up to its sum insured, and after payment they adjust between themselves by **contribution** — the insurer who has paid more than its rateable share recovers the excess from the others (Sec 82 MIA; **North British and Mercantile v. London, Liverpool and Globe, 1877** — the King and Queen Granaries case, where the merchant's insurer and the wharfinger's insurer covered different interests, so no contribution). The **conditions** for contribution: the same subject matter; the same peril; the same interest (a bailee's policy and an owner's policy do not contribute); each policy in force at the time of loss and covering the loss; and the policies not excluding contribution. **Rateable proportion**: by sums insured (the 'maximum liability' method), or by independent liability in liability insurance. Every fire policy contains a **contribution condition** that the insurer pays only its rateable proportion, so the insured must claim from each; and a **'non-contribution' or 'other insurance' clause** that the policy does not cover what is covered elsewhere — when two such clauses meet, courts cancel them out and both insurers contribute (Gale v. Motor Union, 1928). **Over-insurance** (insuring above value with one insurer) is simply wasted premium: the insured recovers only the value, and in valued policies the agreed value binds unless fraudulent. **United India Insurance v. Levi Strauss (India) (2022)** — where the same goods were covered by a global policy in favour of the parent and a local fire policy, the insured could not recover twice; contribution and indemnity applied."));
c.push(H3("Subrogation"));
c.push(P("**Subrogation** is the insurer's right, on paying the insured's loss, to step into the insured's shoes and enforce all his rights and remedies against third parties in respect of the loss (Sec 81 MIA 1963; **Castellain v. Preston**). It is a corollary of indemnity: the insured must not be paid twice (by the insurer and by the wrongdoer), and the wrongdoer must not escape because the victim was insured. Rules: (1) it arises only in **indemnity insurance** — never in life or personal accident (the widow keeps both the policy and the damages); (2) it arises only **after payment** in full of the loss under the policy (the insurer cannot sue before paying), though the policy usually contains a clause allowing the insurer to act before; (3) the insurer sues **in the insured's name** (or joins him), and is limited to what the insured could recover and to what the insurer has paid — any surplus belongs to the insured (Yorkshire Insurance v. Nisbet Shipping, 1962); (4) the insured must not prejudice the right — a release of the wrongdoer after the loss makes the insured liable to the insurer (Phoenix v. Spooner, 1905), and Indian policies say so expressly; (5) the right covers rights under contracts (carriers' liability, guarantees), torts and statutes, and even gifts made to reduce the loss (Castellain); (6) subrogation is distinct from **assignment**: an assignee sues in his own name for the whole claim; a subrogated insurer sues in the insured's name for what it paid — **Economic Transport Organisation v. Charan Spinning Mills (2010) 4 SCC 114** (Constitution Bench): a 'subrogation-cum-assignment' letter given by the insured lets the insurer sue in its own name; a consumer complaint by the insured with the insurer as co-complainant is maintainable; the earlier view in Oberai Forwarding Agency v. New India (2000) that the insurer alone cannot be a 'consumer' was explained. **Statutory subrogation** in motor and employer's liability insurance — Episode 10."));
c.push(H3("Reinsurance"));
c.push(P("**Reinsurance** is insurance of the insurer: the original insurer (the cedant) transfers part of its risk to a reinsurer for a share of the premium, keeping its full liability to the original insured, who has no privity with the reinsurer (Delver v. Barnes, 1807). It spreads catastrophe risk, increases underwriting capacity and stabilises results. **Kinds**: facultative (case by case) and treaty (automatic cession of a class of business); proportional (quota share, surplus) and non-proportional (excess of loss, stop loss). The reinsurer 'follows the fortunes' of the cedant and the contract is one of utmost good faith. **Indian law**: Sec 101A of the Insurance Act requires every Indian general insurer to reinsure a percentage of the sum assured on each policy with an 'Indian reinsurer' (the GIC, made the national reinsurer in 2000) — the obligatory cession, now four per cent under IRDAI orders; Sec 101B lets the IRDAI fix the terms; the 2015 amendment allowed foreign reinsurers to open branches (Sec 2(9)(d), 3) and Lloyd's to operate in India; the IRDAI (Re-insurance) Regulations 2018 set the order of preference (Indian reinsurers, then foreign branches, then cross-border reinsurers). Reinsurance also governs the insurer's solvency margin and its retention limits (Sec 64VA — assets to exceed liabilities by the prescribed margin)."));
c.push(CASE("Leyland Shipping Co v. Norwich Union Fire Insurance (1918) AC 350","Proximate cause is the dominant, efficient cause, not the last in time; the torpedo, not the tides, sank the Ikaria; the war exclusion applied."));
c.push(CASE("Pawsey v. Scottish Union and National (1907, PC)","Proximate cause defined as the active, efficient cause setting in motion a train of events without a new independent intervening force."));
c.push(CASE("Marsden v. City and County Assurance (1865) LR 1 CP 232","Plate glass broken by a mob that gathered because of a neighbouring fire: proximate cause riot, not fire; the fire exclusion in the glass policy did not apply, the insured recovered."));
c.push(CASE("New India Assurance v. Zuari Industries (2009) 9 SCC 70","Fire in the lubrication system was the proximate cause of the turbo-generator's destruction though an explosion intervened; the insurer's 'explosion is excluded' defence failed."));
c.push(CASE("Sarbati Devi v. Usha Devi (1984) 1 SCC 424","A nominee under Sec 39 merely receives the money; the heirs own it — now subject to Sec 39(7) beneficial nominees (2015)."));
c.push(CASE("Economic Transport Organisation v. Charan Spinning Mills (2010) 4 SCC 114","Subrogation vs assignment explained; a subrogated insurer with an assignment may sue in its own name; consumer complaint by insured and insurer together maintainable against the carrier."));
c.push(CASE("North British and Mercantile v. London, Liverpool and Globe (1877) 5 Ch D 569","Contribution arises only where the policies cover the same interest; the owner's insurer and the bailee's insurer covered different interests and did not contribute."));
c.push(DIG("Subrogation recoveries in motor claims are now pursued through the insurers' inter-company 'knock-for-knock' agreements and the IRDAI's motor claims portal; the Insurance Information Bureau flags double insurance on the same vehicle or property at the proposal stage."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Proximate cause', 'Leyland Shipping case', 'Subrogation', 'Contribution', 'Double insurance', 'Reinsurance', 'Nomination vs assignment', 'Sarbati Devi'. Part B: 'Explain the doctrine of causa proxima with illustrations from decided cases' — the definition, Sec 57 MIA, the four tests with Leyland, Marsden, Wayne Tank, Zuari; or 'Explain subrogation and contribution as corollaries of indemnity, their conditions and limits' — Castellain, Yorkshire v. Nisbet, Economic Transport, North British, Levi Strauss; or 'Distinguish nomination from assignment under the Insurance Act with reference to the 2015 amendment'. Part C: a ship torpedoed and later sunk by tides — war peril (Leyland); a fire in a shop leads to looting — riot, not fire (Marsden); a factory insured for ten lakh with A and twenty lakh with B suffers a six-lakh loss — the insured may claim all from either; A and B contribute two lakh and four lakh; an insurer pays the insured for goods damaged by a carrier and the insured then releases the carrier — insured liable to the insurer; a widow who is the nominee of a 2016 policy is asked by the deceased's brother to share — she keeps it (Sec 39(7)); a nominee of a 2010 policy — holds for all heirs (Sarbati Devi)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — The Man Who Hid His Illness: Mithoolal Nayak and the Law of Life Insurance"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Nature and scope of life insurance · Definition (Sec 2(11)) · Kinds of life insurance · The policy and formation of the contract · Conditions of life policies · Circumstances affecting the risk · Assignment and nomination · Amounts recoverable · Persons entitled to payment · Settlement of claims and payment of money · The LIC Act 1956 and the modern industry"));
c.push(STORY("Madhya Bharat, 1943. Mahajan Deolal, a trader of about fifty, proposed for a life policy of twenty-five thousand rupees with the Empire of India Life Assurance Company. The first proposal, in 1943, was declined after the company's doctor examined him; the doctor noted anaemia, a poor chest and signs that suggested tuberculosis. Deolal did not give up. In 1944 he proposed again, to the same company, through a different agent, and was examined by a different doctor; he answered 'no' to every question about previous illness, previous treatment, and previous proposals declined, and the policy was issued in 1945 on a life 'accepted at ordinary rates'. Within months he was seriously ill; he died in November 1946 of pulmonary tuberculosis. His assignee Mithoolal Nayak claimed the twenty-five thousand rupees. By then the Life Insurance Corporation stood in the company's shoes. It repudiated: the proposer had been treated for the very disease that killed him, had been declined by the same insurer for it, and had denied both. The Supreme Court held that all three requirements of the then Sec 45 were made out — the statements were on material matters, they were fraudulently made, and Deolal knew they were false — and the policy was void; the premiums were forfeited. The case is remembered for the three-condition test, and for the observation that a proposal form is not a formality but the foundation on which the insurer decides whether to accept a life. The 2015 rewriting of Sec 45 has changed the time-limits and shifted the burden, but Mahajan Deolal's answers would fail even the new section: they were fraud within three years."));
c.push(H3("Nature and scope"));
c.push(P("**Life insurance** is a contract by which the insurer, in consideration of a premium, undertakes to pay a sum of money on the death of the person whose life is insured or on his surviving a stated period. **Sec 2(11) Insurance Act**: 'life insurance business' means the business of effecting contracts of insurance upon human life, including any contract whereby the payment of money is assured on death (except death by accident only) or on the happening of any contingency dependent on human life, and any contract subject to payment of premiums for a term dependent on human life; it includes the granting of disability and double or triple indemnity accident benefits, annuities upon human life, and superannuation allowances and benefit annuities. Features: (1) it is not a contract of indemnity but a **contingency contract** — the sum is payable in full; (2) the event (death) is certain, only its time is uncertain, so it is also **a form of saving** (except pure term insurance); (3) **insurable interest** is required at inception only; (4) it is a long-term contract with a **level premium** — the excess of early premiums over the cost of the risk builds a reserve, which is why the policy acquires surrender and paid-up values and can be borrowed against; (5) the risk is of the person, so age, health, occupation and habits are the material facts; (6) it is regulated by the Insurance Act 1938, the LIC Act 1956, the IRDA Act 1999 and the IRDAI's product, policyholder-protection and distribution regulations."));
c.push(H3("Kinds of life insurance"));
c.push(TBL([
["Kind","What it does","Notes"],
["Term (temporary) assurance","Pays only if death occurs within the term; nothing on survival","Cheapest; pure protection; 'return of premium' variants; convertible term"],
["Whole life","Pays on death whenever it occurs; premiums for life or a limited period","Protection plus estate; participating (with-profits) or non-participating"],
["Endowment","Pays on death within the term or on survival at the end","Saving plus protection; the LIC's classic policy; money-back is an endowment with periodic survival benefits"],
["Anticipated endowment / money-back","Part of the sum paid at intervals; full sum on death","Liquidity"],
["Joint life","Two lives (spouses, partners); pays on the first death","Or on the last death"],
["Annuity","Insured pays a lump sum or instalments; insurer pays a periodic sum for life","Immediate or deferred; the reverse of life insurance; pension products"],
["Unit-linked (ULIP)","Premium buys units in a fund; sum assured plus fund value","Investment risk on policyholder; IRDAI (Unit Linked Insurance Products) Regulations; SEBI-IRDAI dispute settled by the 2010 ordinance in IRDAI's favour"],
["Group life","Master policy covering employees, members, borrowers","Employer-employee, group gratuity, group credit life; PMJJBY (two lakh cover for ₹436)"],
["Children's, education, pension plans; riders","Accident benefit, critical illness, waiver of premium, term riders","Riders limited by IRDAI to a percentage of the base premium"],
["Micro-insurance","Small sums, rural and social sector obligations (Sec 32B-32C)","IRDAI (Micro Insurance) Regulations"]
],[2200,3600,3200]));
c.push(H3("The policy and formation of the contract"));
c.push(P("Formation follows Episode 01: proposal (with personal statement, medical report where required, agent's confidential report), acceptance by the insurer's competent authority communicated to the proposer (**Raja Vasireddy**), first premium (**Sec 64VB**; the proviso lets the risk run from the date of the proposal if the premium was paid then), and the policy. **Contents of the policy**: the schedule (policy number, name and age of the life assured, date of commencement, sum assured, premium and mode, term, maturity date, nominee), the operative clause (the promise to pay), the conditions and privileges, and the endorsements. IRDAI's regulations require a **free-look period** of thirty days (from 2024, for all policies) within which the policyholder may return the policy and get a refund less the cost of risk cover and stamp duty. **Age** is a material fact and must be proved (admitted) — Sec 45(5) allows adjustment for misstatement of age at any time; the policy usually says the sum will be adjusted to the premium paid if the age was understated and the excess refunded if overstated. **Days of grace, lapse, revival, paid-up and surrender values** — as in Episode 03; **Sec 113** of the Insurance Act (as amended) makes a policy acquire a guaranteed surrender value after premiums have been paid for two consecutive years, and the IRDAI regulations fix the minimum. **Loans** against the policy are available up to a percentage of the surrender value, usually with an assignment in favour of the insurer."));
c.push(H3("Conditions of life policies"));
c.push(P("The standard conditions: (1) **proof of age** — production of a birth certificate, school certificate, passport, etc.; (2) **payment of premium** and grace; (3) **forfeiture** — non-payment, but subject to non-forfeiture (paid-up value after the minimum period); (4) **revival** of a lapsed policy within the period the policy allows on proof of continued good health and payment of arrears with interest — a revival is a fresh contract for the purpose of disclosure and Sec 45's three years run again from the revival; (5) **suicide clause** — if the life assured commits suicide within twelve months of commencement or revival, the policy is void and the insurer pays only 80 per cent of premiums (IRDAI's linked and non-linked product regulations now require this refund; the older clause paid nothing); (6) **accident benefit** exclusions — intentional self-injury, insanity, intoxication, war, aviation other than as a passenger, criminal acts; (7) **restrictions on travel, occupation and residence** — now largely dropped; (8) **assignment and nomination** provisions; (9) **claims** conditions — proof of death, title of the claimant, discharge; (10) **indisputability** — the Sec 45 bar restated; (11) **'basis of the contract'** declaration in the proposal; (12) **grace and lapse**. Conditions must be construed strictly against the insurer and the insured must be told of them (IRDAI requires a 'key features document' and the policy in the language of the proposal)."));
c.push(H3("Circumstances affecting the risk"));
c.push(P("The insurer classifies lives as standard, sub-standard (rated up — extra premium, lien on the sum for a period, exclusion) or declined, on: **age** (mortality rises with age; misstatement is the most common dispute); **health** — present and past illnesses, family history, height and weight, blood pressure, sugar, habits (tobacco, alcohol), the results of medical examination; **occupation** (hazardous — mining, aviation, defence, chemicals); **residence and travel**; **sex** (women's mortality is lower; premium differentials are permitted); **moral hazard** — a proposal for a sum out of proportion to income, or by a person other than the life assured with a doubtful interest; **previous insurance history** — proposals declined, postponed or rated (a material fact: Rekhaben); **existing policies** (aggregate exposure). The proposer must disclose all these; a change between proposal and acceptance must be disclosed; after acceptance, changes of occupation, residence or health need not be (unless the policy says so) — a policy on a healthy clerk who later becomes a soldier and dies in war is payable unless war is excluded."));
c.push(H3("Assignment and nomination — recap and interplay"));
c.push(P("**Assignment (Sec 38)** transfers the policy — absolutely or conditionally — and the assignee alone can claim; **nomination (Sec 39)** designates the recipient on death, and the nominee holds for the heirs unless a beneficial nominee under Sec 39(7). An assignment cancels a nomination (Sec 39(4)), except an assignment to the insurer for a loan or a conditional assignment that reverts; a nomination is not cancelled by a will; the policyholder's marriage does not affect either. A policy under **Sec 6 of the Married Women's Property Act 1874** — effected by a man on his own life for the benefit of his wife and/or children — creates a trust; the money does not form part of his estate, is beyond his creditors, and is paid to the trustees named; it cannot be assigned or nominated by him later. Where the policy is a **keyman policy**, the employer is the proposer and beneficiary; on the employee's leaving it may be assigned to him."));
c.push(H3("Amounts recoverable"));
c.push(P("On **death**: the sum assured with accrued bonuses (participating policies — reversionary bonus declared yearly from the surplus disclosed by the actuarial valuation under Sec 13, terminal bonus), plus accident benefit if death was by accident, less any policy loan with interest and unpaid premiums within the grace period; on **maturity**: the sum assured with bonuses, or the fund value for ULIPs; on **surrender**: the surrender value; on **lapse** after the minimum period: the paid-up value. Interest is payable on delayed claims (IRDAI: two per cent above bank rate for delays beyond the settlement period). **Persons entitled to payment**: the policyholder on maturity or surrender; on death — the assignee if assigned; the nominee (beneficial or as receiver); the trustees under a MWP Act policy; failing these, the legal heirs or representatives — the insurer may require a succession certificate (Sec 370 of the Indian Succession Act) or letters of administration, or may pay on an indemnity bond for small sums; Sec 45ZA-type provisions do not exist for insurance — the nominee under Sec 39 fills the role. Where a minor is entitled, payment is to the guardian or appointee."));
c.push(H3("Settlement of claims and payment of money"));
c.push(P("**Death claims**: intimation, the claim form, the death certificate (Sec 12 of the Registration of Births and Deaths Act), the policy, proof of title, hospital and medical certificates where death was within the early years (early-death claims are investigated), the employer's certificate, the police report and post-mortem in accident cases. The IRDAI (Protection of Policyholders' Interests, Operations and Allied Matters of Insurers) Regulations 2024 (replacing the 2017 regulations) require the insurer to settle or reject a death claim within **fifteen days** of receiving all documents where no investigation is needed, and within **forty-five days** where an investigation is warranted (the older limit was thirty and ninety days); delay attracts interest. A **repudiation** must be in writing with reasons (Sec 45(2)); the claimant may approach the insurer's grievance cell, then the **Insurance Ombudsman** (claims up to fifty lakh rupees, Insurance Ombudsman Rules 2017 as amended 2021 — the award binds the insurer), or the **consumer commission** under the Consumer Protection Act 2019 (deficiency in service — LIC v. Consumer Education and Research Centre; the fora regularly award interest and compensation for wrongful repudiation), or a civil suit (Art 44 Limitation Act — three years from the date of death or the date the money became due). **Maturity claims**: the insurer must send a notice and discharge voucher in advance and pay on the due date. Payment is by electronic transfer to the claimant's account (IRDAI requires bank details to be collected at proposal). **LIC's special statutory guarantee**: Sec 37 of the LIC Act 1956 — the sums assured by all LIC policies, including bonuses declared, are guaranteed as to payment in cash by the Central Government."));
c.push(H3("The LIC Act 1956 and the industry after 1999"));
c.push(P("The **Life Insurance Corporation Act 1956**: Sec 3 constitutes the Corporation; Sec 4 the Board; Sec 5 capital (five crore rupees, raised to one hundred crore in 2011 and restructured in 2021); Sec 6 the function — to carry on life insurance business to the best advantage of the community and to develop it, and Sec 6(2) the powers; Sec 7 vested all controlled business of the existing insurers in it; Sec 21 Central Government directions; Sec 24-28 the Life Insurance Fund, audit and surplus (95 per cent to policyholders as bonus, now 90 per cent after the 2021 changes); Sec 30 gave the LIC the **exclusive privilege** of carrying on life insurance business in India — deleted by the IRDA Act 1999, which let private companies register; Sec 37 the Government guarantee; Sec 43 applied specified sections of the Insurance Act to the LIC. The 2021 amendments (Finance Act 2021) reorganised the LIC as a company-like body with share capital, an independent board and a listing — the IPO of May 2022. The **IRDA Act 1999**: Sec 3 the Authority; Sec 14 its duties, powers and functions — registration, protection of policyholders' interests (nomination, assignment, insurable interest, settlement of claims, surrender value), regulating conduct, agents, surveyors, solvency margins, investments, adjudicating disputes between insurers and intermediaries, controlling rates for tariff business; Sec 26 regulations; Sec 30-32 amended the Insurance Act, LIC Act and GIBNA. Today 26 life insurers operate (LIC with about 60 per cent of premium), under product regulations that fix minimum sum assured, surrender values, commission caps (Sec 40), the rebating prohibition (Sec 41 — offering a rebate of commission is an offence), and Sec 42's licensing of agents (now appointed by insurers under the 2015 amendment)."));
c.push(CASE("Mithoolal Nayak v. LIC, AIR 1962 SC 814","Fraudulent concealment of tuberculosis and an earlier declined proposal; policy void; the three conditions of the old Sec 45; premiums forfeited."));
c.push(CASE("LIC v. Consumer Education and Research Centre (1995) 5 SCC 482","LIC's Table 58 policy, restricted to salaried persons in government and reputed firms, violated Art 14; LIC as 'State' must act fairly and reasonably; insurance is a social security measure; contracts of adhesion are subject to fairness."));
c.push(CASE("LIC v. Asha Goel (2001) 2 SCC 160","Sec 45 conditions; the insurer must prove fraudulent suppression on a material matter; repudiation within the two-year (now three-year) window; writ maintainable against LIC."));
c.push(CASE("Delhi Electric Supply Undertaking v. Basanti Devi (1999) 8 SCC 229","Under a salary-savings scheme the employer is LIC's agent for collecting premium; failure to remit does not lapse the policy; LIC must pay, with recourse against the employer."));
c.push(CASE("Alka Shukla v. LIC (2019) 6 SCC 64","Accident benefit rider: death by heart attack while driving a scooter is not death by 'accident'; the accident must be the proximate cause; the rider was not payable, the basic sum was."));
c.push(CASE("Shakti Yezdani v. Jayanand Jayant Salgaonkar (2023) SCC OnLine SC 1679","Nominee holds for the heirs (Sarbati Devi followed); nomination does not override succession; noted the 2015 beneficial-nominee exception under Sec 39(7) of the Insurance Act."));
c.push(DIG("Since 2016 life policies may be held in an e-Insurance Account with a repository (CDSL, NSDL, Karvy, CAMS); claims are filed online; LIC's 'Jeevan Saakshya' and the IRDAI's Bima Bharosa grievance portal track turn-around times; death certificates are verified against the Civil Registration System; and the 2024 regulations require insurers to accept claim intimation through any channel and to decide within the fifteen/forty-five-day limits."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Life insurance business (Sec 2(11))', 'Endowment vs whole life', 'ULIP', 'Suicide clause', 'Days of grace', 'Surrender value', 'Sec 37 LIC Act', 'Free-look period', 'Beneficial nominee'. Part B: 'Explain the nature and kinds of life insurance and the formation of a life insurance contract' — definition, features (not indemnity, interest at inception), the table of kinds, formation with Raja Vasireddy and Sec 64VB; or 'Discuss the conditions of a life policy and the circumstances affecting the risk'; or 'Explain the law relating to settlement of claims under a life policy and the remedies of a policyholder against wrongful repudiation' — documents, time-limits, Sec 45, Ombudsman, consumer forum, Asha Goel. Part C: a policyholder commits suicide in the ninth month — void, 80 per cent of premium refunded; a claim on a revived policy is repudiated for a fact concealed at revival two years earlier — permissible, revival restarts Sec 45; the employer deducted premium but did not remit — LIC must pay (Basanti Devi); the nominee is the mother but the policy was assigned to a bank for a loan — bank first, then the nominee; the insurer delays a death claim eight months — interest and compensation in the consumer forum."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — The Jewels in the Grate: Harris v Poland and the Law of Fire Insurance"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Definition and scope · Nature of the fire insurance contract · Meaning of 'fire' · Formation · Insurable interest · Indemnity and reinstatement · Causa proxima in fire · Kinds of fire policies · Conditions — alteration, notice of loss, abandonment, average · Rights of the insurer after loss · Amount recoverable"));
c.push(STORY("London, 1940. Mrs Harris was afraid of burglars. Before going out one evening she wrapped her jewellery and some banknotes in a parcel and hid them in the grate of her sitting-room fireplace, under the unlit fuel — no burglar, she reasoned, would look there. She came home cold and forgetful, and lit the fire. By the time she remembered, the notes were ash and the jewels were ruined. Her policy covered 'loss or damage caused by fire'. The insurers, Poland and others at Lloyd's, said this was not a fire loss at all: the fire was where a fire ought to be, doing what a fire ought to do; nothing had happened to the fire, only to the jewels. Atkinson J. disagreed. The policy insured against loss caused by fire, not against loss caused by a fire that had got out of control; the jewels were damaged by fire; and the loss was fortuitous — the accident was that the jewels were in the grate, not that the fire was. The insurers paid. Harris v Poland is the neatest illustration of two things every fire-insurance answer needs: the meaning of 'fire' (actual ignition, of something not meant to burn, or burning where it was not meant to burn) and the requirement of fortuity — the loss must be accidental so far as the insured is concerned, though it may be quite deliberate as far as the fire is concerned."));
c.push(H3("Definition and scope"));
c.push(P("**Sec 2(6A) Insurance Act**: 'fire insurance business' means the business of effecting, otherwise than incidentally to some other class of insurance business, contracts of insurance against loss by or incidental to fire or other occurrence customarily included among the risks insured against in fire insurance policies. A **contract of fire insurance** is one by which the insurer, for a premium, undertakes to indemnify the insured against loss or damage to specified property by fire (and the other named perils) during a specified period, up to a specified sum. Its scope covers buildings and their contents, plant and machinery, stock and stock-in-process, furniture, fixtures — everything except what the policy excludes (money, securities, documents, jewellery unless declared, explosives, standing crops, land, vehicles). In India the **Standard Fire and Special Perils Policy** (the tariff form of 1987 as revised, still the basis after de-tariffing; since 2021 the IRDAI's standard products 'Bharat Griha Raksha' for homes, 'Bharat Sookshma Udyam Suraksha' and 'Bharat Laghu Udyam Suraksha' for small enterprises) covers twelve perils: fire; lightning; explosion or implosion; aircraft damage; riot, strike and malicious damage; storm, cyclone, typhoon, tempest, hurricane, tornado, flood and inundation; impact damage; subsidence and landslide; bursting or overflowing of water tanks and pipes; missile testing operations; leakage from automatic sprinklers; bush fire — with add-ons for earthquake, terrorism, spontaneous combustion, forest fire and loss of profits (consequential loss / business interruption)."));
c.push(H3("Nature of the fire insurance contract"));
c.push(P("(1) A contract of **indemnity** (Castellain v. Preston) — the insured recovers the actual loss up to the sum insured, never more; hence subrogation, contribution, salvage and average. (2) A contract of **utmost good faith** — construction, occupation, previous fires and previous refusals are material. (3) A **personal contract** — it insures the insured, not the property; it does not pass with the property (Rayner v. Preston) unless assigned with consent, though Sec 49 TPA gives the buyer a right to have the money applied to reinstatement. (4) A contract for **one year** ordinarily, renewable, with the duty of disclosure reviving at renewal. (5) **Insurable interest** must exist at inception and at the time of loss — the owner, the mortgagee, the bailee, the tenant, the purchaser once risk passes, the hypothecatee bank (Canara Bank v. United India, 2020). (6) The **loss must be fortuitous** — accidental as regards the insured; the insured's own negligence does not defeat the claim (Shaw v. Robberds, 1837; Harris v. Poland; Sikka Papers), but his wilful act (arson) does, and the insurer bears the burden of proving it on a preponderance of probability with the seriousness of the charge kept in mind."));
c.push(H3("Meaning of 'fire'"));
c.push(P("**Fire means actual ignition** — combustion with flame or glow; smoke, heat or scorching without ignition is not fire. **Austin v. Drewe (1816)**: sugar in a refinery was spoiled when a flue register was left shut and the heat, without any fire escaping the flue, damaged the sugar — no loss by fire, because the fire stayed where it should and nothing burned that should not. **Stanley v. Western Insurance (1868)**: 'fire' in the policy means fire that has escaped from its proper place or is burning something not intended to be burnt; loss by explosion is not fire loss unless caused by fire. **Harris v. Poland (1941)**: jewels burnt in a grate — the fire was in its proper place but burnt what was not meant to be burnt; fire loss. **Everett v. London Assurance (1865)**: concussion from a gunpowder explosion half a mile away broke the insured's windows — not fire damage. So a fire is (a) actual ignition, (b) of something not intended to burn, or (c) in a place where it was not intended to be, (d) fortuitous as regards the insured. **Consequences of fire** are covered as fire loss: damage by smoke, by water used to extinguish, by demolition to stop the spread, by fire brigades, by the fall of walls, by removal of goods from the burning building (Levy v. Assicurazioni, 1940). **Not covered unless added**: loss by explosion (unless the explosion is caused by fire, or is of domestic boilers or gas — the standard policy now includes 'explosion/implosion' as a peril); spontaneous combustion (heating of the insured property from its own fermentation — cotton bales, coal), which is excluded in the standard policy unless added; the 'burning of property by order of a public authority'; and loss by theft during or after the fire (excluded expressly). **Lightning** is a separate peril and covers damage even without ignition."));
c.push(H3("Formation and the policy"));
c.push(P("The proposal form asks for the property (address, construction of walls, roof and floors — 'class of construction'), occupation, adjoining hazards, the value and the sum insured, previous losses and refusals, existing insurance; the insurer may inspect. The **cover note** (Chandmull Jain) runs until the policy issues. **The policy**: the schedule (insured, address, sum insured item-wise, period, premium), the operative clause ('the company agrees that if after payment of the premium the property insured or any part thereof shall be destroyed or damaged by any of the perils specified hereunder during the period of insurance the company shall pay to the insured the value of the property at the time of the happening of its destruction or the amount of such damage or at its option reinstate or replace such property'), the exclusions, the general conditions (fifteen in the standard form), the warranties and endorsements. Warranties in fire insurance (no storage of hazardous goods, sprinklers maintained, watchman) are strictly enforced but read against the insurer."));
c.push(H3("Indemnity and reinstatement"));
c.push(P("**Indemnity**: the measure is the value of the property at the time and place of loss, not its cost when bought nor its replacement cost — for buildings, the cost of repair less depreciation; for machinery, the market value of a similar used machine; for stock, cost or market value, whichever is lower; for household goods, second-hand value; plus the cost of removing debris where covered. Consequential loss (loss of profits, rent, wages) is excluded unless a **loss of profits policy** is taken. **Reinstatement**: (a) the insurer's **option to reinstate** — the standard policy lets the insurer choose to reinstate or replace instead of paying; once it elects to reinstate it must do so completely even if the cost exceeds the sum insured (Brown v. Royal Insurance, 1859), and the insured must give plans and assistance; in England Sec 83 of the Fires Prevention (Metropolis) Act 1774 lets any interested person require the insurer to spend the money on reinstatement — there is no Indian equivalent except Sec 49 TPA for transferees and the mortgagee's rights under Sec 72; (b) the **reinstatement value clause** — an endorsement under which the insurer pays the cost of rebuilding or replacing new-for-old, without deduction for depreciation, provided the property is actually reinstated within a reasonable time (twelve months) and the sum insured represents the reinstatement value; if not reinstated, indemnity on the ordinary basis. (c) **Salvage**: on payment of a total loss the insurer takes the salvage."));
c.push(H3("Causa proxima in fire insurance"));
c.push(P("The insured peril must be the proximate cause (Episode 04). Illustrations: property removed from a burning building and damaged by rain in the street — fire (the removal was a reasonable consequence); goods stolen during the confusion — theft, excluded; a wall weakened by the fire falls a week later — fire; an explosion caused by fire — fire (and the standard policy covers explosion anyway); a fire caused by an explosion — the explosion is proximate (Stanley v. Western — now academic since both are covered; but a fire caused by an earthquake is excluded unless the earthquake add-on is taken — 'loss by fire caused by earthquake' is an express exclusion); fire caused by riot — riot is proximate, covered under the standard policy but excluded if 'war, invasion, civil commotion assuming the proportions of a popular rising' applies; spontaneous combustion of coal that then sets the godown alight — the standard exclusion removes only the self-heated property, the rest is fire loss (a fine distinction examiners like). **Burden**: the insured proves loss by an insured peril; the insurer proves an exclusion. **Sikka Papers v. National Insurance (2009)**: burning of waste paper in the open on the insured's premises spread to the stock; the insurer said the insured had breached a warranty and caused the fire; held — the loss was by fire, negligence is no defence, the warranty was not proved to have been breached; but the sum was reduced for under-insurance."));
c.push(H3("Kinds of fire policies"));
c.push(TBL([
["Policy","Description"],
["Specific","A fixed sum on each item; pays loss up to that sum without regard to value (no average unless the average clause is inserted — it always is now)"],
["Valued","The value of the property is agreed in the policy; on total loss the agreed value is paid regardless of market value; used for works of art, rare goods"],
["Valuable / unvalued","The value is assessed after loss — the ordinary policy"],
["Average","Contains the pro rata condition of average — under-insurance reduces the claim proportionately"],
["Floating","One sum covering goods lying at several locations or fluctuating between them (e.g. stock in several godowns) at one rate; subject to average"],
["Declaration","For fluctuating stocks: a provisional premium on a maximum sum, monthly declarations of actual stock, premium adjusted at year-end; loss paid on the declared stock"],
["Reinstatement value","New-for-old, subject to actual reinstatement"],
["Excess / first loss","Excess: covers only above a specified amount; first loss: covers only up to a fraction of the value because total loss is unlikely (large warehouses), with waiver of average"],
["Comprehensive / package","Fire plus burglary, machinery breakdown, public liability, etc. in one policy (householder's package, shopkeeper's package)"],
["Consequential loss (loss of profits)","Covers reduction in turnover, standing charges and increased cost of working during the indemnity period after a fire; requires a material damage policy in force"],
["Sprinkler leakage, blanket, adjustable, transit","Special forms; 'blanket' covers all property at all locations for one sum"]
],[2600,6400]));
c.push(H3("Conditions of fire policies"));
c.push(P("The standard policy's general conditions: (1) **Misdescription** — the policy is voidable for material misdescription or non-disclosure. (2) **Alteration** — the cover ceases (unless the insurer agrees by endorsement) if the trade or manufacture is altered, the building becomes unoccupied for more than thirty days, or the **interest passes** from the insured otherwise than by will or operation of law; this is the 'alteration' condition the syllabus mentions. (3) **Marine policies** — the fire policy does not cover property insured by a marine policy except for the excess. (4) **Cancellation** — by the insurer on fifteen days' notice with pro rata refund, by the insured at short-period rates. (5) **Loss** — on the happening of loss the insured must forthwith give **notice** to the insurer, and within **fifteen days** (or such further time as the insurer allows) deliver a claim in writing with particulars of the property and the amount, proofs and information; he must not abandon property to the insurer — **'no property may be abandoned to the company'** unless the company consents — this is the 'notice of abandonment' point in fire insurance (contrast marine, where abandonment is a right); if the claim is fraudulent, or a wilful act of the insured, all benefit is forfeited. (6) **Right of entry** — on the happening of loss the insurer may enter the premises, take possession of the property, examine, sort, remove and sell the salvage; but is not thereby bound to undertake reinstatement or liable for damage. (7) **Reinstatement** option. (8) **Average**. (9) **Contribution**. (10) **Subrogation** — the insured must give the insurer all assistance to recover from third parties before and after payment. (11) **Arbitration** — disputes on quantum (not liability) go to arbitration (the 'Scott v. Avery' clause: arbitration is a condition precedent). (12) **Time bar** — the claim is deemed abandoned if a suit or arbitration is not commenced within twelve months of rejection; the courts read this against forfeiture where the insurer never clearly rejected (National Insurance v. Sujir Ganesh Nayak, 1997 — a clause extinguishing the claim after twelve months is valid and not hit by Sec 28 Contract Act as it extinguishes the right, not merely the remedy; the 1997 amendment to Sec 28 was intended to change this for future contracts). (13) **Reasonable precautions**. (14) **Notice of other insurance**. (15) **Cover for 'risks of debris removal', architects' fees** up to limits."));
c.push(H3("Average"));
c.push(P("The **condition of average** (pro rata condition of average): if at the time of loss the property is collectively of greater value than the sum insured, the insured is considered his own insurer for the difference and bears a rateable share of the loss. Formula: claim = loss × (sum insured ÷ value at risk). A godown with stock worth ten lakh insured for five lakh suffers a two-lakh loss: the insurer pays one lakh. Purpose: to make under-insurance unattractive and premiums fair. Applies item by item in specific policies; floating policies and declaration policies are always subject to average; a 'waiver of average' up to 15 per cent under-insurance is common in Indian tariffs. Average does not apply where the sum insured is adequate, nor to valued policies. **Sikka Papers** applied it to reduce the claim."));
c.push(H3("Rights of the insurer after loss; amount recoverable"));
c.push(P("**After the loss the insurer may**: enter and take possession (condition 6); investigate — appoint a **licensed surveyor** under **Sec 64UM** of the Insurance Act, which requires a surveyor's report for claims above the threshold fixed by the IRDAI (currently one lakh rupees for non-motor claims) and lets the insurer pay more or less than the surveyor's assessment for recorded reasons — the survey report is not binding but cannot be ignored without cogent grounds (**Sri Venkateswara Syndicate v. Oriental Insurance, 2009**; repeated surveys are not allowed to whittle down a claim); require proofs and documents; elect to reinstate; take the salvage; exercise subrogation; avoid the policy for fraud or breach; refer quantum to arbitration. **Amount recoverable**: the actual loss (value at the time and place of loss, item by item), less salvage, less any excess, subject to the sum insured (item-wise), to average, to contribution, and to the policy limits for debris removal and fees; plus interest on delayed payment (IRDAI requires settlement within thirty days of the survey report, with interest thereafter). A claim inflated fraudulently forfeits the whole (condition 8); an honest overestimate does not. The insured must **mitigate** — reasonable expenses of protecting the property from further loss are recoverable."));
c.push(CASE("Harris v. Poland (1941) 1 KB 462","Jewels hidden in a grate and burnt when the fire was lit: loss 'caused by fire'; fire need not be out of control; the loss was fortuitous as regards the insured."));
c.push(CASE("Austin v. Drewe (1816) 6 Taunt 436","Sugar spoiled by heat from a flue with no ignition outside it — not a loss by fire."));
c.push(CASE("Stanley v. Western Insurance (1868) LR 3 Ex 71","Fire means ignition; explosion not caused by fire is not fire; the two are distinct perils."));
c.push(CASE("Castellain v. Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380","Fire insurance is indemnity; the vendor who received the full price after a fire holds the insurance money for the insurers."));
c.push(CASE("Sikka Papers Ltd v. National Insurance (2009) 7 SCC 777","Fire from burning of waste on the premises spread to the stock: negligence no defence; the insurer must prove a breach of warranty; average applied for under-insurance."));
c.push(CASE("Sri Venkateswara Syndicate v. Oriental Insurance (2009) 8 SCC 507","Surveyor's report under Sec 64UM is the basis of settlement; the insurer cannot appoint surveyor after surveyor to reduce a claim; it may depart from the report only for valid reasons."));
c.push(CASE("Canara Bank v. United India Insurance (2020) 3 SCC 455","A bank that financed the insured stock and was named as loss payee/hypothecatee had a direct claim as a beneficiary; the insurer bound by the survey and by the Consumer Protection Act's definition of consumer."));
c.push(CASE("Oriental Insurance v. Sony Cheriyan (1999) 6 SCC 451","The policy is to be read strictly; where it insured a chemical tanker for use with permitted goods and the vehicle carried inflammable goods without a permit, the insurer was not liable."));
c.push(DIG("Fire claims are now surveyed with drone and photo evidence uploaded to the insurer's portal; IRDAI's surveyor regulations (2015, amended 2020) fix the threshold for mandatory survey and the timelines — the surveyor must report within thirty days (extendable), and the insurer must decide within thirty days of the report. The IRDAI's standard 'Bharat' fire products (2021) fix policy wording for homes and small enterprises, include a fifteen per cent waiver of under-insurance and cover twelve perils in plain language."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Meaning of fire', 'Harris v. Poland', 'Average clause', 'Reinstatement', 'Floating policy', 'Declaration policy', 'Loss of profits policy', 'Sec 64UM', 'Alteration condition'. Part B: 'Define fire insurance and explain the nature of the contract, the meaning of fire and the rule of proximate cause as applied to fire claims' — Sec 2(6A), the six features, Austin, Stanley, Harris, Everett, the consequences covered, Zuari and Sikka; or 'Explain the conditions usually found in a fire policy and the rights of the insurer after loss'; or 'Write notes on kinds of fire policies and the condition of average'. Part C: goods spoiled by heat from a furnace whose door was left open — no ignition outside, not fire (Austin), unless smoke damage after an escape; stock insured for four lakh worth eight lakh, loss two lakh — pays one lakh; a shop sold to a buyer without endorsement burns — the seller's policy has ceased (interest passed), the buyer has no policy; the insured throws stock out of the window during a fire and it is stolen — fire covers the damage from throwing, theft is excluded; the insurer's surveyor assesses six lakh and the insurer pays three without reasons — the consumer forum orders six with interest (Venkateswara Syndicate)."));
c.push(PB());

module.exports=c;
